{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 8, :comments ["<b>Who are ready</b> — that they are prepared to rouse their mourning, as in \"וְעָתַדָה בַשָּׂדֶה לָךְ\" and \"וְעֲתִידוֹתֵיהֶם שׁוֹסִיתִי.\" The nun of לויתן is in place of a mem, and its meaning is: mourning. And in the words of our Rabbis of blessed memory: \"a woman shall not rouse her לויתה at the appointed time.\" Some say: it refers to men of a ship that was about to break apart, who cursed the day they boarded it, for they are destined to be eaten by Leviathan, which is a species of great fish. But the nearer [meaning] is that cursers of the day are eulogizers in every tongue, who curse the days."], :book :job, :voice :ibn-ezra, :lang :en, :chapter 3, :notes {0 "The two biblical citations (1 Samuel 8:16 and Micah 2:2) are quoted in fragmentary Hebrew without full context; rendered as they stand. The Aramaic/Hebrew hybrid 'לויתה' is retained as Ibn Ezra cites it from rabbinic literature."}, :source-witnesses [["Ibn Ezra on Job -- Daat" "http://www.daat.ac.il/he-il/tanach/parshanut_hamikra/full-text/eben-ezra/eyov/"]], :timestamp "2026-07-09T19:01:40.082467673Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}